WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:00.000 --> 00:00:04.960
Film Courage: What factors into you saying yes&nbsp;
to a project and what factors into you saying no?

00:00:04.960 --> 00:00:07.120
Brad Rushing, Cinematographer:&nbsp;
Let me limit my answer to a&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:07.680 --> 00:00:12.640
narrative (to scripted narrative) because&nbsp;
there's so many other manifestations but&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:14.240 --> 00:00:17.760
the one thing I would say is that&nbsp;
it's just got to be a great script.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:20.240 --> 00:00:26.960
I've learned the hard way that that no amount&nbsp;
of talent behind or in front of the camera,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:26.960 --> 00:00:33.600
no amount of money can compensate for a bad&nbsp;
script. Meanwhile you can take a really,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:33.600 --> 00:00:41.760
really good script and and have shabby technical&nbsp;
values and still have a really entertaining movie&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:42.800 --> 00:00:50.800
so I look at what I do as as window dressing you&nbsp;
know and I think it's important of course but but&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:50.800 --> 00:00:57.840
if I don't have the skeleton to hang my&nbsp;
pretty pictures on then it's just gonna be&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:59.360 --> 00:01:06.240
vacuous so the first thing is that is that I&nbsp;
want to see a good script the other thing is&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:06.240 --> 00:01:12.800
that I want to know that these people have&nbsp;
enough money to do justice to that script&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:13.600 --> 00:01:18.080
you know I'm not going to be biased if&nbsp;
you come at me with you know 10 million&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:18.080 --> 00:01:24.560
dollars or if you come at me with 200,000 as&nbsp;
long as you can do a good job on your script&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:25.200 --> 00:01:32.080
with that money if you're being unrealistic to&nbsp;
me that's like buying a ticket on the titanic&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:32.080 --> 00:01:36.720
and I do not want to go down with it because&nbsp;
I'll be blamed you know if there aren't the&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:36.720 --> 00:01:42.960
resources for the crew if there aren't enough&nbsp;
days if there are unrealistic expectations&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:42.960 --> 00:01:48.160
if there are tons of visual effects and not enough&nbsp;
money to pay for them it's going to look like crap&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:48.160 --> 00:01:53.840
and my name's going to be on it I don't care&nbsp;
how much they pay me I'm not going to do it&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:55.360 --> 00:02:00.160
so so you know a good script and&nbsp;
the ability to do a good job with it&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:01.280 --> 00:02:06.800
and then I look at the collaborators you know&nbsp;
who is the director who is the producer who are&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:06.800 --> 00:02:11.920
the other keys the production designer&nbsp;
who's the talent you know the the cast&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:13.280 --> 00:02:18.000
and if there are people I'm excited to&nbsp;
work about if they've done or if I'm if&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:18.000 --> 00:02:22.400
there are people I'm excited to work with&nbsp;
if they've done good work if I know them&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:23.760 --> 00:02:29.520
you know if they give me a really compelling&nbsp;
pitch I mean all of that you know can&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:31.200 --> 00:02:37.440
can play to getting me excited and you know&nbsp;
just like with the money I wouldn't know that&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:37.440 --> 00:02:41.520
they have enough time I don't want them to tell&nbsp;
me they're going to do a movie in three days&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:41.520 --> 00:02:45.840
you know because that's again I probably&nbsp;
should have said resource instead of money I&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:45.840 --> 00:02:50.640
just want to make sure they have the resources&nbsp;
enough money enough crew enough time and that's&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:50.640 --> 00:02:54.640
not saying you have to have a ton of any of&nbsp;
those things you don't have to have a ton&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:54.640 --> 00:03:00.160
of money you don't have to have a ton of crew&nbsp;
or a ton of days but you know you can't have&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:00.160 --> 00:03:08.480
a shoot with three location moves in a day and two&nbsp;
crew people and you know it's just unless you're&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:08.480 --> 00:03:13.200
shooting guerrilla style in which case I mean&nbsp;
that's a legitimate form of filmmaking but for&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:13.200 --> 00:03:19.760
me as a cinematographer what does that do for me&nbsp;
I mean does that show anything that I want to show&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:19.760 --> 00:03:25.120
there are some DP's who will embrace that because&nbsp;
that's their jam and that's wonderful but for me&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:25.120 --> 00:03:32.080
I'm a very you know painterly and interactive I&nbsp;
mean I want to affect the image and if I don't&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:32.080 --> 00:03:36.720
feel I'm you know if I feel to me that would&nbsp;
be damage control if there's too many location&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:36.720 --> 00:03:41.840
moves and it's just like set up an exposure set&nbsp;
up the camera focus get an exposure shoot move&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:42.480 --> 00:03:49.920
I'm like where's the creativity for me and that&nbsp;
you know again somebody else maybe it's there&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:49.920 --> 00:03:56.080
but for me I don't necessarily feel it's there and&nbsp;
I might be wrong somebody might pitch me a movie&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:56.080 --> 00:04:01.200
and convince me oh yeah here you know you can do&nbsp;
it this way in that way again this is the whole&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:01.200 --> 00:04:06.320
malleable thinking I mean I'm okay well I would I&nbsp;
would break that rule because you've convinced me&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:07.600 --> 00:04:11.120
but just as a general thing&nbsp;
I want to see that I can&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:12.480 --> 00:04:15.920
practically achieve something I'm&nbsp;
going to be proud to put my name on&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:16.480 --> 00:04:22.240
and won't be embarrassed by and then you know&nbsp;
the last thing is is money you know I mean&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:24.320 --> 00:04:31.600
is it a friend asking me to come help out on a&nbsp;
weekend is it a movie asking me to go to Brazil&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:31.600 --> 00:04:39.120
and shoot for two months you know what is it and&nbsp;
so you know I would I would I would consider that&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:39.120 --> 00:04:42.560
and if it's something I really really want to&nbsp;
do well I at least have to make enough to make&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:42.560 --> 00:04:50.480
my bills you know but ideally you know we want to&nbsp;
make enough to have some savings and and have fun&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:50.480 --> 00:04:57.200
and especially when I do travel jobs you know I&nbsp;
like I like bringing you know Kathy with me and&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:57.840 --> 00:05:04.240
or you know and then we have some vacation&nbsp;
days and you know like if we go to Canada or or&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:05:04.800 --> 00:05:09.920
you know wherever just you know turn it into&nbsp;
a little mini holiday because most of the&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:05:09.920 --> 00:05:17.760
traveling I've been all over the world I've been&nbsp;
to every continent except Antarctica and it's 95&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:05:18.480 --> 00:05:24.000
work travel and what I do is I'll build&nbsp;
in extra days you know I'll stay a little&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:05:24.000 --> 00:05:28.880
longer or I'll go early or you know if there's&nbsp;
a break in the middle but I turn it into these&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:05:28.880 --> 00:05:37.520
little vacations and you know it's just super fun&nbsp;
have you ever turned down work and then when you&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:05:37.520 --> 00:05:45.040
looked back on it realized maybe it was a mistake&nbsp;
oh yes I have a great story and this is a money&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:05:45.040 --> 00:05:53.040
thing too and it goes the opposite way of what&nbsp;
I've been saying so yeah I had a friend of mine.

00:05:55.280 --> 00:06:02.960
A casting director who I really got along with&nbsp;
and he had cast some movies that I shot and he&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:06:02.960 --> 00:06:06.000
pitched me he said you know&nbsp;
Don Coscarelli the guy who did&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:06:06.000 --> 00:06:11.760
Phantasm is doing a horror movie and it's&nbsp;
a horror comedy and and they would like you&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:06:12.560 --> 00:06:17.440
to shoot it and it was this movie called&nbsp;
Bubba Ho-Tep and I thought well that sounds&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:06:17.440 --> 00:06:22.640
fun because you know I like ancient Egypt&nbsp;
and I like horror and I like comedy and they&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:06:22.640 --> 00:06:26.800
called me up and we talked and I said oh that's&nbsp;
great and they said you know what's the rate&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:06:28.560 --> 00:06:34.640
and they said 400 and I'm like okay 400 a day&nbsp;
I can you know I can make that work and I said&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:06:34.640 --> 00:06:38.800
okay yeah four hundred dollars a day is fine and&nbsp;
they said no it's four hundred dollars a week.

00:06:41.280 --> 00:06:47.280
And either this was right when I was starting&nbsp;
music videos or right before but anyway I was&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:06:47.280 --> 00:06:54.560
making a lot of money at the time and I'm just&nbsp;
like I'm just like I just can't I can't see that&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:06:54.560 --> 00:07:01.280
happening you know and I turned it down and I&nbsp;
was fine with that well turned out that they&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:07:01.280 --> 00:07:09.120
cast Bruce Campbell and Aussie Davis in it you&nbsp;
know two amazing legendary actors and it's a cult&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:07:09.120 --> 00:07:16.640
classic and and and I just kick myself I'm like oh&nbsp;
my goodness I wish I had done that movie you know&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:07:16.640 --> 00:07:24.000
I just in retrospect I don't give a shout. I would&nbsp;
have done it for free but but you just never know&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:07:24.000 --> 00:07:30.960
I mean I've I've done movies that I took a chance&nbsp;
on and then they came out crappy I don't know I&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:07:32.160 --> 00:07:37.280
hey that was that's just my experience that&nbsp;
was that was one that I wish I had made a&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:07:37.280 --> 00:07:43.840
different decision but but when I looked at the&nbsp;
cards on the table they just didn't make sense.

